WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.580 --> 00:00:09.730
Have you ever sympathized with a monster?

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:15.700
I remember as a child seeing images depicting
the legend of Saint George slaying a dragon.

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:19.850
But I didn’t have the reaction to these
pictures I was supposed to.

00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:22.070
The dragons didn’t look evil.

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:23.480
They looked sad.

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:29.331
And the older I’ve become, the more media
I’ve seen where monsters suffer…

00:00:29.331 --> 00:00:31.634
the less monstrous they seem.

00:00:31.634 --> 00:00:38.323
(Sympathy for the Monster)

00:00:38.323 --> 00:00:44.539
To empathize with a monster is to go against thousands of years of storytelling precedent.

00:00:44.539 --> 00:00:49.530
The traditional mythic role of a monster is
to be a kind of living obstacle, typically

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:53.410
one that embodies dominant fears in its culture
of origin.

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:58.660
The very word ‘Monster’ comes from the
Latin ‘monere,’ meaning ‘to warn.’

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:03.899
When a courageous hero vanquishes a monster,
it’s not supposed to be tragic, but a victory

00:01:03.899 --> 00:01:06.510
over a societal anxiety.

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:11.020
This conventional model of monster-hood is
one most of us are familiar with.

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:13.640
Next to — pottery, maybe?

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:17.520
— monsters are about as close to a cultural
universal as you can get.

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:21.450
Wherever there are people telling stories,
there are stories of monsters.

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:27.159
But what is considered monstrous and worthy
of scorn or fear varies across cultures and

00:01:27.159 --> 00:01:28.159
time.

00:01:28.159 --> 00:01:34.000
So, what one civilization judges to be irredeemable,
another might pity and empathize with.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:35.790
It all comes down to perspective.

00:01:35.790 --> 00:01:40.520
Like, there’s that one scene in Star Wars
where our hero crushes a monster called a

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:45.840
Rancor with a gate, which feels triumphant,
until you see this one side character looks

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:49.329
so heartbroken, like this creature meant the
world to him.

00:01:49.329 --> 00:01:55.049
And though the Rancor didn’t seem like the…
nicest fellow, as a child this moment made

00:01:55.049 --> 00:02:02.350
me wonder if the creature was truly evil,
or just a starving animal in a pit.

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:06.659
Sympathizing with a monster isn’t a novel
concept, but it is one that’s evolved over

00:02:06.659 --> 00:02:07.770
the years.

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:12.709
One of the most important turning points in
film came in the early 20th century when Universal

00:02:12.709 --> 00:02:18.120
Pictures thrust the tragic monster into the
Hollywood zeitgeist with the success of the

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:19.300
1931’s Frankenstein.

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:25.450
Based on Mary Shelly’s 19th century novel
about scientist Victor Frankenstein who animates

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:30.590
a creature made from corpses, the film makes
numerous changes to the source material, yet

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:34.830
keeps the core tragedy of the central unwanted
creation.

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:40.050
Upon awakening, Frankenstein’s monster is
essentially an innocent newborn, but because

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:44.800
of his monstrous strength and appearance,
he’s abandoned and mistreated by those around

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:46.940
him — including his own creator.

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:52.110
It is through this mistreatment that the monster
learns to act monstrous, lashing out with

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:55.660
anger at a world that has given him anger
in turn.

00:02:55.660 --> 00:03:00.459
And the film emphasizes that the creature
does have the capacity for kindness if met

00:03:00.459 --> 00:03:04.040
with kindness, even if just for a fleeting
moment.

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:08.769
Though he cannot help being inhuman in the
eyes of others, his nature is not inherently

00:03:08.769 --> 00:03:10.049
inhumane.

00:03:10.049 --> 00:03:15.690
Where the novel and the film differ is that
in the movie, the creature remains infantile

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:20.970
until the end, whereas in the book he becomes
eloquent and cunning, committing inhumane

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:24.269
acts with full awareness to punish his creator.

00:03:24.269 --> 00:03:29.550
Yet the novel, too, indicates that the creature’s
cruelty stems from its initial abandonment,

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:34.769
and the horror that follows is ultimately
the fault of Dr. Frankenstein’s hubris.

00:03:34.769 --> 00:03:39.790
The very idea for the creature came from Mary
Shelly’s apprehension towards people attempting

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:42.510
to use electricity to revive corpses.

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:48.730
So, while Frankenstein’s monster embodies
fears of human recklessness, he is also, innately,

00:03:48.730 --> 00:03:51.120
a victim of that same recklessness.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:55.769
There’s the old ‘gotcha’ that “Frankenstein
isn’t the name of the monster, but the name

00:03:55.769 --> 00:03:56.879
of the man.”

00:03:56.879 --> 00:04:02.120
But from a thematic perspective, it’s not
that clear cut…

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:08.700
Yet Frankenstein wasn’t the only 19th-century-monster-novel-turned-blockbuster
to come out in 1931.

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:14.409
Universal’s Dracula was an even bigger hit
— sticking more closely to a folkloric model

00:04:14.409 --> 00:04:20.030
of monster-hood, with a largely unsympathetic
monster threatening the sanctity of a social

00:04:20.030 --> 00:04:21.030
good.

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:26.870
This Dracula can be seen as a manifestation
of U.S. postwar anxieties over perceived external

00:04:26.870 --> 00:04:32.490
dangers coming from afar to threaten the home,
as can many of the Universal creature-features

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:33.680
that followed.

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:38.870
Yet a surprising number of these films also
find pathos in their monsters, with the writers

00:04:38.870 --> 00:04:44.400
aware, at least on some level, of the tragedy
inherent in being rejected by society for

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:46.460
something you can’t control.

00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:51.310
So maybe it’s no surprise that many of these
monsters have been reimagined over the years

00:04:51.310 --> 00:04:54.400
as more sympathetic and non-threatening figures.

00:04:54.400 --> 00:05:00.810
Asking audiences to empathize with, say, a
vampire, is far from unheard of these days.

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:09.139
“It opened its wings like this, screeching,
now you are vampire!

00:05:09.139 --> 00:05:12.440
…And it was Peter.

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:17.780
And we are still friends today.”

00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:23.220
The surprisingly sympathetic framing of Universal’s
early monsters can in many ways be traced

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:27.419
back to the 1928 silent film The Man Who Laughs.

00:05:27.419 --> 00:05:32.750
The story of a circus performer mutilated
as a child into having a permanent grin, the

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:38.910
central figure in The Man Who Laughs is neither
a supernatural creature, nor in any way villainous.

00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:43.330
He is only a ‘monster’ from the perspective
of a public that will not accept him.

00:05:43.330 --> 00:05:48.440
Though likely the inspiration for the iconic
villain the Joker, the original man who laughs

00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:52.699
never snaps and becomes the monster everyone
expects him to be.

00:05:52.699 --> 00:05:57.030
In fact, he gets a happy ending despite his
appearance never changing.

00:05:57.030 --> 00:06:03.530
For a movie from the 1920s, the film is surprisingly
nuanced in its exploration of the lead character’s

00:06:03.530 --> 00:06:05.530
lived experience.

00:06:05.530 --> 00:06:10.039
At the time of release, the man who laughs
was lumped with figures like the Hunchback

00:06:10.039 --> 00:06:15.570
of Notre Dame by marketers under the umbrella
of ‘Universal Monsters’ — which speaks

00:06:15.570 --> 00:06:20.270
to a callous public perception of people that
were physically non-normative.

00:06:20.270 --> 00:06:25.560
This tragically echoes the history of many
so-called monsters in folklore, with werewolf

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:31.220
legends, for example, likely related to individuals
with hypertrichosis — a condition that causes

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:33.420
hair growth throughout the body.

00:06:33.420 --> 00:06:38.840
Fear of the unknown or of that which a social
majority deems abnormal is at the heart of

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:44.020
a lot of monster myths, with the label of
‘monster’ used to demonize and dehumanize

00:06:44.020 --> 00:06:47.530
those perceived as ‘other.’

00:06:47.530 --> 00:06:53.590
In European history, fear of the unknown also
manifested in the form of ‘geographic monsters’

00:06:53.590 --> 00:06:58.500
— the kinds of beasts that populate the
edges of Medieval and Renaissance maps, meant

00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:02.340
to invoke the perceived dangers of far-off
locations.

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:07.630
Over the centuries, tall-tales of fantastical
beings from distant lands and distorted accounts

00:07:07.630 --> 00:07:13.620
of actual wildlife combined with growing Imperialism
into a worldview that linked monsters with

00:07:13.620 --> 00:07:15.660
regions unmapped by Europeans.

00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:21.130
By the early 20th century, this worldview
merged with paleontological discoveries to

00:07:21.130 --> 00:07:26.410
create ‘Lost World’ narratives, wherein
adventurers would discover not mythic monsters

00:07:26.410 --> 00:07:30.970
but lost prehistoric life — which they then
‘conquered’ in a manner that reflected

00:07:30.970 --> 00:07:35.160
imperialist attitudes towards nature and indigenous
populations.

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:40.680
And out of this muddled mythos emerged King
Kong — one of the more enduring pop-culture

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:47.250
figures of the last century, with a complex
place in the realm of sympathetic monster-dom.

00:07:47.250 --> 00:07:52.250
Coming two years after Universal’s monsters
broke onto the scene, the original King Kong

00:07:52.250 --> 00:07:54.330
is a movie of contrasts.

00:07:54.330 --> 00:07:59.990
On the one hand, the titular Kong feels like
the geographic monster personified — presented

00:07:59.990 --> 00:08:05.250
as a treacherous embodiment of the unknown
that the intrepid explorers must subjugate.

00:08:05.250 --> 00:08:10.979
On the other hand, unlike most Universal monsters,
Kong is — at least initially — not the

00:08:10.979 --> 00:08:14.120
invading figure, but the one attacked in his
own home.

00:08:14.120 --> 00:08:20.150
And the film does seem aware of and sympathetic
to this unfairness on some level.

00:08:20.150 --> 00:08:25.311
Not just an obstacle, Kong is given moments
that could be classified as heroic — which

00:08:25.311 --> 00:08:28.720
later reboots have made more explicitly valiant.

00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:34.430
Though Kong eventually rampages through New
York, this only occurs after he is forcibly

00:08:34.430 --> 00:08:36.409
removed from his original environment.

00:08:36.409 --> 00:08:41.899
And here the film’s sympathies split once
again, as Kong’s eventual fate at the hands

00:08:41.899 --> 00:08:47.730
of the military is presented as both tragic…
and the preordained outcome of nature clashing

00:08:47.730 --> 00:08:49.750
with industrial society.

00:08:49.750 --> 00:08:55.140
Peter Jackson’s reboot in particular brings
this tragedy more to the surface, but in that

00:08:55.140 --> 00:09:00.650
film, Kong’s defeat upon challenging humanity
still seems inescapable.

00:09:00.650 --> 00:09:05.279
Which is why it’s notable that the latest
incarnation of Kong flips this dynamic on

00:09:05.279 --> 00:09:06.279
its head.

00:09:06.279 --> 00:09:12.220
In that series, Kong is closer to a deity
— a symbolic reminder of how insignificant

00:09:12.220 --> 00:09:16.290
humanity is compared to the endurance of the
natural world.

00:09:16.290 --> 00:09:22.310
This is also the most overtly noble version
of Kong ever put to film: presented as an

00:09:22.310 --> 00:09:25.220
honorable defender of the ecosystem.

00:09:25.220 --> 00:09:31.050
The original movie might pity Kong to an extent,
but the latest incarnation is explicitly,

00:09:31.050 --> 00:09:35.300
textually on the side of Kong and not the
humans pillaging his home.

00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:38.290
He is always the invaded — never the invader.

00:09:38.290 --> 00:09:42.730
And I think this transformation means more
than critics give it credit for.

00:09:42.730 --> 00:09:49.060
The fact that Kong has gone from semi-antagonist
to… essentially an action hero, suggests

00:09:49.060 --> 00:09:56.060
that the attitude of general audiences towards
monsters has changed over the last century.

00:09:56.060 --> 00:10:00.740
Speaking of which, isn’t there another iconic
giant monster who’s gone from villain to

00:10:00.740 --> 00:10:02.959
hero over the decades?

00:10:02.959 --> 00:10:09.430
Godzilla rose up from the depths in the early
50s, ushering in a new wave of atomic age

00:10:09.430 --> 00:10:10.490
monsters.

00:10:10.490 --> 00:10:15.500
Anxiety over the seemingly limitless potential
of nuclear power led to a slew of films where

00:10:15.500 --> 00:10:19.340
unfeeling mutants terrorized humanity for
their folly.

00:10:19.340 --> 00:10:24.000
Though many were the creation of Hollywood,
Godzilla was a uniquely Japanese depiction

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:29.540
of post-nuclear trauma, with the first film
a somber analogue for atomic devastation.

00:10:29.540 --> 00:10:36.140
But the more Godzilla films came out, the
more the tone, uh, shifted a little bit — with

00:10:36.140 --> 00:10:38.760
the movies becoming increasingly silly.

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:44.310
No longer a dead-serious embodiment of postwar
suffering, Godzilla became something of a

00:10:44.310 --> 00:10:50.670
national mascot — a figure who, though occasionally
still destructive, was typically contrasted

00:10:50.670 --> 00:10:53.970
against a more overtly villainous monster.

00:10:53.970 --> 00:10:58.870
This change was in response to Japanese audiences
sympathizing with the character, and wanting

00:10:58.870 --> 00:11:02.990
to see him as the protagonist, despite his
monstrous form.

00:11:02.990 --> 00:11:09.100
The director of the original Godzilla, Ishirō
Honda, has a poignant quote on this, stating:

00:11:09.100 --> 00:11:11.230
“Monsters are tragic beings.

00:11:11.230 --> 00:11:14.750
They are born too tall, too strong, too heavy…

00:11:14.750 --> 00:11:17.589
They are not evil by choice.

00:11:17.589 --> 00:11:21.360
That is their tragedy.”

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:26.320
Over the decades, Godzilla’s villainy would
wax and wane through different incarnations

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:31.990
— but one of the most important incarnations
came recently in the film Shin Godzilla.

00:11:31.990 --> 00:11:38.200
This movie, in many respects, returns Godzilla
to his roots as a personification of wartime

00:11:38.200 --> 00:11:39.200
destruction.

00:11:39.200 --> 00:11:44.529
It’s probably the most nightmarish version
of Godzilla seen onscreen thus far, a borderline

00:11:44.529 --> 00:11:48.090
eldritch being who brings only devastation.

00:11:48.090 --> 00:11:52.920
But hidden within the narrative is a highly
sympathetic portrait of an animal in a state

00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:53.980
of suffering.

00:11:53.980 --> 00:11:59.639
Godzilla begins the film in apparent agony
— a mutant newly created from nuclear waste

00:11:59.639 --> 00:12:01.180
barely able to stand.

00:12:01.180 --> 00:12:06.209
And no matter how destructive he becomes,
there’s always a sense this is a creature

00:12:06.209 --> 00:12:07.810
in terrible pain.

00:12:07.810 --> 00:12:14.270
During the final rampage, ominous operatic
music plays, but if you listen to the lyrics,

00:12:14.270 --> 00:12:20.100
it seems like a plea from Godzilla’s perspective,
lamenting that no one understands its sorrow.

00:12:20.100 --> 00:12:24.440
“If I die in this world, / Who will know
something of me?

00:12:24.440 --> 00:12:27.560
/ …A downward slope / Is all I see.”

00:12:27.560 --> 00:12:33.490
Shin Godzilla isn’t about to save the world
with a majestic dropkick, yet the film argues

00:12:33.490 --> 00:12:37.709
this veritable god of ruin deserves your sympathy
as well.

00:12:37.709 --> 00:12:43.130
Like Mary Shelly’s monster, he is a victim
of humanity’s hubris, a monster who never

00:12:43.130 --> 00:12:45.480
asked to be made.

00:12:45.480 --> 00:12:50.620
Conversely, a heroic modern interpretation
of the character comes from the Legendary

00:12:50.620 --> 00:12:56.040
films, wherein Godzilla is not a mutant, but
a preexisting superorganism from the Permian

00:12:56.040 --> 00:12:59.670
Period that nuclear testing happened to awaken.

00:12:59.670 --> 00:13:05.279
This changes the character into more of a
force of nature rather than manmade calamity.

00:13:05.279 --> 00:13:11.240
Much like the latest version of Kong, Godzilla
is presented as a guardian of ecological stability

00:13:11.240 --> 00:13:15.410
— in fact, those two incarnations of the
characters share continuity.

00:13:15.410 --> 00:13:21.480
A curious byproduct of this reimagining, however,
is that like earlier films that framed Godzilla

00:13:21.480 --> 00:13:27.420
as the protagonist, these monsters now have
‘monsters’ of their own — unsympathetic

00:13:27.420 --> 00:13:31.740
living obstacles that must be put down to
protect a greater good.

00:13:31.740 --> 00:13:36.899
Typically, these villains take the form of
outside threats to the ecosystem — most

00:13:36.899 --> 00:13:40.779
traditionally, aliens.

00:13:40.779 --> 00:13:45.620
Aliens rose to prominence in Hollywood in
the late fifties and early sixties, stealing

00:13:45.620 --> 00:13:49.589
the crown from nuclear mutants as the dominant
pop-culture monsters.

00:13:49.589 --> 00:13:54.720
So, the fact that the earliest Godzilla films
to frame him as a hero put him up against

00:13:54.720 --> 00:13:59.829
aliens is appropriate — turning him into
the reigning champion of monster-hood defending

00:13:59.829 --> 00:14:02.130
the throne from new upstarts.

00:14:02.130 --> 00:14:08.600
In the U.S. especially, the alien craze reflected
Cold War fears of invasion — the extraterrestrials

00:14:08.600 --> 00:14:13.790
portrayed as infiltrators with secretly deviant
appearances and agendas.

00:14:13.790 --> 00:14:18.160
Aliens might not seem like they belong to
the monster category, but though cloaked in

00:14:18.160 --> 00:14:24.130
the scientific jargon of the space age, most
mid-20th century extraterrestrials were still

00:14:24.130 --> 00:14:30.620
archetypal boogeymen — manifestations of
cultural paranoia that needed to be vanquished.

00:14:30.620 --> 00:14:34.050
Not all aliens to come out of this era were
malicious.

00:14:34.050 --> 00:14:39.190
But aliens that could be trusted typically
looked like Spock, while anything nonhumanoid

00:14:39.190 --> 00:14:41.980
was more likely to be duplicitous.

00:14:41.980 --> 00:14:44.040
This is a trend that’s continued…

00:14:44.040 --> 00:14:45.670
pretty much until the modern day.

00:14:45.670 --> 00:14:50.020
There’s ET, of course, but he’s something
of an outlier.

00:14:50.020 --> 00:14:55.130
Notable recent works that break from this
framing include District 9 and Arrival.

00:14:55.130 --> 00:15:00.279
The design of the aliens in these films fall
in line with the bug or squid-like aliens

00:15:00.279 --> 00:15:06.631
that terrorized the B-movie of the 50s and
60s, yet neither of these non-human aliens

00:15:06.631 --> 00:15:08.480
are depicted as villainous.

00:15:08.480 --> 00:15:13.550
In fact, the central conflict of both films
stems not from the aliens possessing secret

00:15:13.550 --> 00:15:18.910
plans to invade, but from humanity’s fearful
reaction to a perceived threat.

00:15:18.910 --> 00:15:23.399
As was often true in the McCarthy era, the
only monsters in these films are the ones

00:15:23.399 --> 00:15:29.460
people invent — and the ones they become
in the name of destroying the imagined interlopers.

00:15:29.460 --> 00:15:36.670
A sympathetic monster who exists at the crossroads
of all these themes is the Gill Man from Universal’s

00:15:36.670 --> 00:15:39.290
The Creature from the Black Lagoon.

00:15:39.290 --> 00:15:44.560
First appearing in the 50s, the Gill Man was
a monster dislodged from time, both in the

00:15:44.560 --> 00:15:47.570
narrative, and in era the film was released.

00:15:47.570 --> 00:15:52.480
Though marketed as a sci-fi creation, the
Gill Man fit more with the then out-of-style

00:15:52.480 --> 00:15:55.209
geographic monster of King Kong’s era.

00:15:55.209 --> 00:16:00.420
And as was the case with Kong, the portrayal
of the Gill Man is a complicated one.

00:16:00.420 --> 00:16:05.511
He is easily the least aggressive of the classic
Universal monsters, beginning the film as

00:16:05.511 --> 00:16:10.670
more curious and only resorting to violence
after being attacked multiple times — and

00:16:10.670 --> 00:16:12.760
in his own home no less.

00:16:12.760 --> 00:16:17.839
As film director Guillermo Del Toro puts it,
“He gets his home invaded and then roughed

00:16:17.839 --> 00:16:19.079
around, you know?

00:16:19.079 --> 00:16:23.089
…He was at home, swimming, and these guys
barge in.”

00:16:23.089 --> 00:16:27.900
Yet the film itself doesn’t fully break
from the Imperialist model of monster-hood,

00:16:27.900 --> 00:16:31.790
with the defeat of the Gill Man framed as
largely triumphant.

00:16:31.790 --> 00:16:36.810
This questionable framing is the reason why
Del Toro’s chose the Gill Man as the inspiration

00:16:36.810 --> 00:16:39.410
for the creature in The Shape of Water.

00:16:39.410 --> 00:16:44.779
Set in Baltimore in the 1960s, the film subverts
the classical monster narrative by making

00:16:44.779 --> 00:16:50.540
the fish man… basically the secondary protagonist
— using his plight as a means of exploring

00:16:50.540 --> 00:16:53.769
the systemic inequality endemic to the era.

00:16:53.769 --> 00:16:58.359
The Shape of Water makes overt what’s been
true for monster-media since its inception

00:16:58.359 --> 00:17:05.140
— that portrayals of monsters often mirror
portrayals of marginalized groups.

00:17:05.140 --> 00:17:10.640
Leading out of the late 60s, pop-culture monsters
would become increasingly decentralized as

00:17:10.640 --> 00:17:17.100
film genres became more diverse, with their
appearance and level of sympathy varying greatly.

00:17:17.100 --> 00:17:21.750
One of the last widespread trends was the
horde of zombie-centric media that shambled

00:17:21.750 --> 00:17:25.140
to the forefront of monster-dom in the 2010s.

00:17:25.140 --> 00:17:31.190
Though the mindless zombie might feel purely
unsympathetic, the myth intertwines with an

00:17:31.190 --> 00:17:36.860
older, particularly tragic form of monster-hood
we’ll call the ‘transformed monster.’

00:17:36.860 --> 00:17:42.400
Stories like The Wolfman and Dr. Jekeil and
Mr. Hyde helped establish the trope of otherwise

00:17:42.400 --> 00:17:46.520
innocent humans who transform into monsters
and lose control.

00:17:46.520 --> 00:17:51.640
In their monster forms, these characters typically
cannot be reasoned with.

00:17:51.640 --> 00:17:56.110
The sympathy comes from the audience knowing
who they were — and recognizing that this

00:17:56.110 --> 00:17:58.850
is a fate they would never choose.

00:17:58.850 --> 00:18:02.460
Mainstream zombies appeal to emotion in a
similar way.

00:18:02.460 --> 00:18:06.940
Though the aggressive creatures have lost
their humanity, there is no escaping the fact

00:18:06.940 --> 00:18:09.490
that each one used to be human.

00:18:09.490 --> 00:18:14.559
Every zombie is the ending of a melancholy
tale, a fact that makes it difficult to completely

00:18:14.559 --> 00:18:21.110
close your heart to them — even if they’re
just enemies in a video game.

00:18:21.110 --> 00:18:26.289
Speaking of which, the most recent and perhaps
largest wave of sympathetic monsters can be

00:18:26.289 --> 00:18:29.039
found in the medium of video games.

00:18:29.039 --> 00:18:33.560
Traditionally, most games have stuck to the
mythic model of monster-hood, portraying them

00:18:33.560 --> 00:18:36.740
as unfeeling enemies that the player must
overcome.

00:18:36.740 --> 00:18:42.299
It’s a format that makes sense for interactivity
— games are all about obstacles, enemies

00:18:42.299 --> 00:18:47.290
make for engaging obstacles, and monsters
are a clear visual shorthand for enemies.

00:18:47.290 --> 00:18:52.460
And yet I think a lot of players can point
to at least one example of feeling bad for

00:18:52.460 --> 00:18:54.050
a virtual monster.

00:18:54.050 --> 00:18:58.650
The first time I encountered this feeling
was when I played Super Mario Galaxy as a

00:18:58.650 --> 00:18:59.880
child.

00:18:59.880 --> 00:19:05.040
Early on in this bright and colorful adventure,
you land on the egg of a giant monster that

00:19:05.040 --> 00:19:07.110
hatches and turns aggressive.

00:19:07.110 --> 00:19:10.500
You then have to defeat this newborn lifeform
in order to progress.

00:19:10.500 --> 00:19:16.080
And it wasn’t until my first time beating
this boss that I realized how sad it is.

00:19:16.080 --> 00:19:21.809
All this creature knows is violence — it’s
literally the first stimulus they ever experience.

00:19:21.809 --> 00:19:25.470
I remember thinking that my actions created
this monster.

00:19:25.470 --> 00:19:28.340
I’d just pulled a Frankenstein!

00:19:28.340 --> 00:19:34.120
…Obviously that’s not what the developers
intended for young-me to take away from this

00:19:34.120 --> 00:19:35.120
game.

00:19:35.120 --> 00:19:40.340
In general, you aren’t supposed to see video
game monsters as thinking, feeling beings.

00:19:40.340 --> 00:19:45.670
Which is why when a developer intentionally
subverts this trope, it can be so powerful.

00:19:45.670 --> 00:19:51.039
The Dark Souls franchise is filled with tragic
enemies, almost everyone under some form of

00:19:51.039 --> 00:19:53.330
heart-wrenching curse or another.

00:19:53.330 --> 00:19:58.070
But easily the most effecting is Sif — a
giant wolf who wields a sword.

00:19:58.070 --> 00:20:03.140
But while this monster might seem ridiculous,
the loyal canine is only trying to protect

00:20:03.140 --> 00:20:05.130
the grave of its deceased owner.

00:20:05.130 --> 00:20:09.690
And to make matters worse, there’s an alternate
route you can take where you bond with Sif

00:20:09.690 --> 00:20:13.090
beforehand, and the wolf recognizes you before
you do battle.

00:20:13.090 --> 00:20:18.809
It makes for an encounter that makes you reevaluate
your role in the story, and wish you could

00:20:18.809 --> 00:20:23.580
choose to break from the cycle of monster
slaying.

00:20:23.580 --> 00:20:28.240
In the game Skyrim… you actually can choose
to break from this cycle.

00:20:28.240 --> 00:20:33.419
In this game, you play as a predestined hero
tasked with defeating a group of villainous

00:20:33.419 --> 00:20:38.550
dragons, and you are rewarded mechanically
for slaying these creatures with useful upgrades.

00:20:38.550 --> 00:20:42.799
For most of the game’s main story, this
conflict is pretty clear-cut — with the

00:20:42.799 --> 00:20:48.190
dragons following the classical, Tolkien-style
model of unambiguous evil.

00:20:48.190 --> 00:20:53.549
But this simplicity is upended when you meet
Paarthurnax, an elderly dragon who has reached

00:20:53.549 --> 00:20:56.940
enlightenment through meditation and now avoids
conflict.

00:20:56.940 --> 00:21:02.500
Part of what makes Paarthurnax so interesting
is he almost seems aware of his metatextual

00:21:02.500 --> 00:21:04.110
role in the story.

00:21:04.110 --> 00:21:09.990
He basically knows it’s his narrative destiny
to be monstrous, and yet he still refuses.

00:21:09.990 --> 00:21:12.409
“What is better?

00:21:12.409 --> 00:21:18.750
To be born good, or to overcome your evil
nature through great effort?”

00:21:18.750 --> 00:21:24.000
And what’s heartbreaking is that you’re
nevertheless tasked with slaying this peaceful

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:28.510
being, and if you succeed, the game rewards
you with greater power.

00:21:28.510 --> 00:21:33.370
But you can choose to never initiate this
fight, and if what I’ve read online is any

00:21:33.370 --> 00:21:34.370
indication, most players don’t.

00:21:34.370 --> 00:21:37.340
Even though the whole game is based around
fighting dragons, like Paarthurnax, people

00:21:37.340 --> 00:21:44.010
seem able to step out of the narrative and
trust empathy over fear.

00:21:44.010 --> 00:21:48.990
Fear is central to the player’s experience
in Shadow of the Colossus, the final game

00:21:48.990 --> 00:21:51.350
I want to discuss in this video.

00:21:51.350 --> 00:21:56.549
The game puts you up against some of the largest
monsters in the medium, creatures so worryingly

00:21:56.549 --> 00:22:00.710
gigantic that all your panicked mind can think
of is how to survive.

00:22:00.710 --> 00:22:05.090
While you might fear them, they certainly
don’t seem to fear you.

00:22:05.090 --> 00:22:09.080
But everything changes when you make it to
the eleventh colossus.

00:22:09.080 --> 00:22:13.850
Not only is this creature relatively tiny
compared to the other behemoths — it’s

00:22:13.850 --> 00:22:15.400
scared of you.

00:22:15.400 --> 00:22:19.380
Most video game enemies don’t show fear
— it’s an emotion that’s a little too

00:22:19.380 --> 00:22:22.370
genuine, a little too human.

00:22:22.370 --> 00:22:26.910
You defeat the eleventh colossus by wielding
a flaming torch to back it off the edge of

00:22:26.910 --> 00:22:27.910
a cliff.

00:22:27.910 --> 00:22:33.360
It’s a moment that, quite intentionally,
makes you question your heroic identity.

00:22:33.360 --> 00:22:37.130
Because though it’s a cliché to say, from
this creature’s perspective…

00:22:37.130 --> 00:22:40.590
the monster is you.

00:22:40.590 --> 00:22:44.990
Humans and monsters have been inexorably intertwined
since our beginning.

00:22:44.990 --> 00:22:49.159
In fact, you could argue humans used to live
in a world of monsters.

00:22:49.159 --> 00:22:54.669
There used to be significantly more megafaunal
species that posed a threat to our ancestors.

00:22:54.669 --> 00:22:59.169
It’s easy to imagine that life in such a
world would have been one of constant fear.

00:22:59.169 --> 00:23:03.470
…But that’s not what you see in ancient
art of these lifeforms.

00:23:03.470 --> 00:23:08.799
You see an incredible amount of care and reverence
for each creature — even ones that they’d

00:23:08.799 --> 00:23:10.799
probably had violent encounters with.

00:23:10.799 --> 00:23:15.490
Though it’s impossible to know the authorial
intent behind prehistoric paintings, when

00:23:15.490 --> 00:23:16.780
I look at these portraits…

00:23:16.780 --> 00:23:21.830
I feel like the artists were aware that these
creatures were alive like they were.

00:23:21.830 --> 00:23:25.440
…I feel like they sympathized with them.

00:23:25.440 --> 00:23:29.940
The main reason why I pitied dragons when
I was little… is because they seemed like

00:23:29.940 --> 00:23:34.279
animals minding their own business until someone
invaded their habitat.

00:23:34.279 --> 00:23:40.460
Indeed, many dragon myths likely stem from
interactions with then-unfamiliar and dangerous

00:23:40.460 --> 00:23:41.460
animals.

00:23:41.460 --> 00:23:45.590
But to my child self, these creatures didn’t
appear threatening — just misunderstood.

00:23:45.590 --> 00:23:50.600
I felt that if someone took the time to document
their behavior, their fate could have been

00:23:50.600 --> 00:23:51.600
avoided.

00:23:51.600 --> 00:23:55.690
In a strange way, I think that’s part of
what set me on the road towards eventually

00:23:55.690 --> 00:23:57.990
making this channel.

00:23:57.990 --> 00:23:59.929
You can’t escape monsters.

00:23:59.929 --> 00:24:05.810
They are culture and history, foundational
to how humans define the personhood of themselves

00:24:05.810 --> 00:24:06.810
and others.

00:24:06.810 --> 00:24:11.550
Though it’s easy to frame sympathy towards
them as a linear increase, the truth is monsters

00:24:11.550 --> 00:24:18.230
are always changing, always representing a
new fear or new avenue for understanding.

00:24:18.230 --> 00:24:24.120
This is actually my 100th Curious Archive
video, and almost every one has featured fictional

00:24:24.120 --> 00:24:26.179
creatures to some extent.

00:24:26.179 --> 00:24:29.029
Monsters aren’t ever going away.

00:24:29.029 --> 00:24:31.690
But we can seek to understand them.

00:24:31.690 --> 00:24:35.900
And as always, thanks for watching.

00:24:35.900 --> 00:24:40.899
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your
support by liking, subscribing, and hitting

00:24:40.899 --> 00:24:45.260
the notification icon to stay up to date on
all things Curious.

00:24:45.260 --> 00:24:57.840
See you in the next video.

